      "paragraphs": {
        "paragraphs": [
          {
            "index": 0,
            "blockIndex": 0,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "To understand wave propagation, we need to follow individual particles of the medium.\nWe begin with a transverse wave traveling along an elastic cord."
          },
          {
            "index": 1,
            "blockIndex": 1,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "Every short segment of the cord has both mass and elasticity.\nWhen the cord bends, elastic forces act across its cross sections."
          },
          {
            "index": 2,
            "blockIndex": 1,
            "paragraphInBlock": 1,
            "text": "The net restoring force tends to pull each segment toward equilibrium.\nInertia carries a moving segment past its equilibrium position."
          },
          {
            "index": 3,
            "blockIndex": 1,
            "paragraphInBlock": 2,
            "text": "Beyond equilibrium, the restoring force slows its outward motion.\nAt maximum displacement it stops briefly, then starts back."
          },
          {
            "index": 4,
            "blockIndex": 2,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "Figure 69 models the cord with a chain of identical metal balls.\nThe balls hang from strings and are joined by springs.\nThe springs have much less mass than the balls."
          },
          {
            "index": 5,
            "blockIndex": 2,
            "paragraphInBlock": 1,
            "text": "Most of the model's mass is concentrated in the balls.\nThe light springs provide elastic coupling.\nThis captures the basic wave mechanism, not every detail of a real cord."
          },
          {
            "index": 6,
            "blockIndex": 5,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "Move the leftmost ball sideways in the horizontal plane.\nHere, sideways means perpendicular to the chain.\nThe connecting spring deforms and pulls the second ball sideways."
          },
          {
            "index": 7,
            "blockIndex": 5,
            "paragraphInBlock": 1,
            "text": "That ball has inertia, so its response takes time.\nIts motion follows the first ball's motion with a delay.\nWe use a small-amplitude model, before appreciable loss or reflection."
          },
          {
            "index": 8,
            "blockIndex": 6,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "Drive the first ball periodically with period $T$, by hand or a mechanism.\nThe second ball is forced to oscillate, with a phase delay."
          },
          {
            "index": 9,
            "blockIndex": 6,
            "paragraphInBlock": 1,
            "text": "Through the next spring, the third ball responds with a further delay.\nThe disturbance continues from ball to ball along the chain."
          },
          {
            "index": 10,
            "blockIndex": 6,
            "paragraphInBlock": 2,
            "text": "After the initial transient, the driven balls share the driving frequency.\nBefore reflected waves return, this is an outgoing transverse wave."
          },
          {
            "index": 11,
            "blockIndex": 7,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "Figure 70 has six overhead views of the ball positions, labeled a, b, c, d, e, and f.\nThe views are a quarter-cycle apart, and each arrow is a ball's velocity vector."
          },
          {
            "index": 12,
            "blockIndex": 8,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "Figure 71a uses heavy balls connected by light springs.\nThe suspension lets each ball oscillate only along the chain.\nDrive the first ball with period $T$ along that same direction."
          },
          {
            "index": 13,
            "blockIndex": 8,
            "paragraphInBlock": 1,
            "text": "A longitudinal wave then travels along the chain.\nClose and wide gaps between balls form compressions and rarefactions.\nFigure 71b shows the pattern as each ball oscillates locally."
          }
        ],
